<launch>
  <!-- RL student-policy inference node (50 Hz), 69D attitude-only. -->
  <!-- SURFACE TEST DEFAULT: thruster_scale=0.0 -> thrusters held at 0, joint-only. -->
  <!-- Brief surface thrust burst (args are declared, so CLI override WORKS):       -->
  <!--   roslaunch albc_rl albc_rl.launch thruster_scale:=1.0 thruster_max_s:=2.0   -->
  <arg name="encoder_type"        default="gru" />    <!-- tcn | gru; shipped pack is 72D gru -->
  <arg name="control_hz"          default="50.0" />
  <arg name="use_board_rates"     default="false" />  <!-- true=trust /albc_status angvel -->
  <arg name="home_on_start"       default="false" />  <!-- ramp the arm to joint1 0,
       joint2 home_joint2 before the policy starts. np_policy seeds its accumulator
       from the MEASURED angles, so the starting pose is an initial condition of the
       run, not a detail, which is why true is the better experiment and false is the
       safer default. FAIL-SAFE default since 2026-08-17: homing COMMANDS the arm and
       cannot unwind it (it stops at the nearest 0-equivalent, then warns and
       proceeds), so it stays opt-in until the restart round-trip regression test
       passes. Pass home_on_start:=true per run once the arm is known unwound. -->
  <arg name="home_joint1"         default="0.0" />
  <!-- 150 deg (2.6179938779914944 rad), NOT pi/2. decision/061 C, 2026-08-26: pi/2
       is max lever (EE radius 0.3295 m, the pose that broke arm1 on 2026-08-22),
       150 deg is 0.1206 m (2.73x less). This is a structural-safety DECISION, not a
       measurement (arm2's allowable stress is still unmeasured), and it is NOT a
       manipulability optimum either (w(150deg)=0.7071 < w(pi/2)=1.0). _home_arm()
       has NO software current protection of its own: the node's over-current
       guard only starts ticking after homing returns (review/058 section 5), so
       this default is the only thing bounding load while homing runs. Previously
       not exposed as a launch arg at all (hardcoded pi/2 in the node). -->
  <arg name="home_joint2"         default="2.6179938779914944" />
  <arg name="home_tol"            default="0.05" />   <!-- rad, _home_arm arrival tol -->
  <arg name="home_timeout_s"      default="25.0" />   <!-- _home_arm gives up (warns,
       proceeds) after this many seconds -->
  <arg name="joint1_start_max_rad" default="3.14159265358979" /> <!-- refuse to start
       the policy if |joint1| exceeds this; <= 0 disables. Sim resets theta1 to
       uniform(-pi, pi) EVERY episode, so the trained +-2-turn budget is measured from
       a near-zero start the real arm does not get for free. -->
  <arg name="joint_delta_scale"   default="0.10" />   <!-- per-TICK joint-target gain,
       np_policy.DELTA_SCALE. Per-second wind rate = this x control_hz, so the two are
       confounded: the 2026-08-13 tank runs calmed 250x at control_hz 10, but that also
       cut the gain 5x. Hold 0.02 at control_hz 50 to isolate observation delay.
       Anything but 0.10 departs from what the checkpoint trained with. -->
  <!-- ARM PROTECTION, added 2026-08-25 after arm2 fractured, current cap + start
       tilt REMAIN below. Defaults are calibrated on the run that broke it
       (.community/posts/finding/047), not guessed, but they are still DECISIONS:
       arm2's allowable stress has never been measured.

       2026-08-26 (decision/061 A1/A2, guard rollback): this block used to also
       carry a joint2 hard window (manipulability threshold w >= 0.3 turned into a
       clamp). REMOVED, not widened: this system is constrained RL and
       singularity avoidance is already a TRAINED cost; the window LATCHED the
       policy mid an ordinary move on 2026-08-25 and forbade half the workspace
       with no argument beyond "unreviewed." See decision/061.

       NOTE for what remains an ANGLE here (home_joint1/2 above): every joint2
       angle in this file depends on the joint2 DATUM (Homing Offset J2 = -61,
       EEPROM addr 20), which RE-SEATING THE ARM MOVES. A 180 deg datum error
       trips loudly; a few degrees is SILENT and shifts every angle together
       while every log line still reads normal. Verify the datum after any
       reassembly (rules-robot-code SKILL.md, rotation-calibration discipline)
       before trusting an angle default here. -->
  <!-- SUSTAINED over-current, not peak: breakaway from rest legitimately draws
       1.1-1.3 A, and the 2026-08-22 arm1 break was 1.0 A SUSTAINED. On the break run
       |I_J2| held above 900 mA for 1.240 s (first at t=4.77 s) while 1000 mA never
       held longer than 0.44 s, so 900/0.5 fires and 1000/0.5 would not.
       <= 0 on either disables.

       2026-08-26: RAISED 900 -> 1500 by operator decision after three tank runs.
       900 was calibrated on ONE transition (holding at rest vs swinging free,
       2026-08-22) and does not cover the load a NORMAL run actually draws:

         J1 azimuth +66 deg, theta2 149.9, at rest, in water .......  304 mA
         J1 azimuth -150 deg, theta2 149.0, at rest, in water ......  707 mA
         target roll 30 deg, holding ...............................  939-1071 mA
         run 3 peak (theta2 swept 130-236 deg) .....................  1547 mA

       The same posture costs 2.3x more current depending on where J1 points,
       so a fixed cap leaves 596 mA of headroom in one azimuth and 193 mA in
       another; and simply commanding target roll 30 deg puts the STATIC load
       above the cap, which is what tripped the 2026-08-26 01:49 run in 0.6 s
       with J1 drawing ~0 mA (the load was entirely J2 holding an attitude, not
       a stall). Two of three runs that night ended on this guard, and neither
       was a stall; the latch froze the arm in the loaded pose and the operator
       had to cut relay power to release it.

       What this buys and what it costs: 1500 clears the measured normal-load
       band (up to 1071 mA holding, 1547 mA peak) so a legitimate run is not
       cut short, and it sits above the 1.0 A SUSTAINED figure from the arm1
       break, so this cap NO LONGER catches a repeat of that break. The
       operator made that trade explicitly. The servo's own overload protection
       is what remains underneath, and it is the layer that already failed once.
       Lower it back toward 1000-1200 for any run that is not attitude-holding.

       The response is still wrong even at 1500: on a trip the guard stops
       commanding and the driver HOLDS the loaded target, so current keeps
       flowing (measured -1150 mA after the 01:26 trip). Detection is not the
       open problem; unloading is. See review/062 and finding/064-065. -->
  <arg name="joint_current_max_ma" default="1500.0" />
  <arg name="joint_current_max_s"  default="0.5" />
  <!-- Refuse to start above this vehicle tilt, sqrt(roll^2+pitch^2) which is
       invariant under rotate_imu. +-30 deg is the attitude envelope the episodes
       sample (cfg/GyroOffset.cfg). The break run started at 50.58 deg and the
       joint1-only start gate passed it. <= 0 disables.

       2026-08-26: RAISED 30 -> 45 by operator decision. 30 was the training
       envelope read straight across as a start limit, so a run measured at
       30.32 deg (roll 30.17, pitch -3.00) was refused over 0.32 deg, and the
       policy does not fall off a cliff at the edge of its sampling range. 45
       still sits below the 50.58 deg that started the 2026-08-25 arm2 break, so
       the one case this gate was built from is still refused.

       What this gives up: between 30 and 45 deg the policy is outside the
       attitude range it ever trained on, so its response there is untested, and
       the failure mode the gate exists to prevent (an untrained maximum-rate arm
       slew from an out-of-envelope start) is now only bounded, not excluded.
       Level the vehicle when you can; this is headroom for the edge, not a
       licence to start tilted. -->
  <arg name="start_att_max_deg"   default="45.0" />
  <arg name="thruster_scale"      default="0.0" />    <!-- 0.0=joint-only surface test, 1.0=live -->
  <arg name="thruster_max_s"      default="0.0" />    <!-- >0 => thrusters latch to 0 after N sec -->
  <arg name="imu_yaw_offset_deg"  default="102.0" /> <!-- CONFIRMED 2026-08-12 (was -78.0, itself 180 deg out; before that 45.0). albc_controller.yaml carries the solve -->
  <arg name="sensor_timeout_s"    default="0.2" />    <!-- IMU staleness gate -->
  <arg name="joint_timeout_s"     default="0.5" />    <!-- /albc/joint_states staleness gate -->
  <arg name="launch_mixer"        default="true" />   <!-- false when the PARENT launch owns
       the mixer. albc_rl_fieldtest.launch includes this file AND runs its own mixer with
       CLI-overridable order/sign/deadband; two nodes named thruster_mixer would collide and
       ROS would silently kill one of them. -->


  <node pkg="albc_rl" name="rl_inference_node" type="rl_inference_node.py" output="screen">
    <param name="encoder_type"       value="$(arg encoder_type)" />
    <param name="control_hz"         value="$(arg control_hz)" />
    <param name="use_board_rates"    value="$(arg use_board_rates)" />
    <param name="joint_delta_scale"  value="$(arg joint_delta_scale)" />
    <param name="home_on_start"      value="$(arg home_on_start)" />
    <param name="home_joint1"        value="$(arg home_joint1)" />
    <param name="home_joint2"        value="$(arg home_joint2)" />
    <param name="home_tol"           value="$(arg home_tol)" />
    <param name="home_timeout_s"     value="$(arg home_timeout_s)" />
    <param name="joint1_start_max_rad" value="$(arg joint1_start_max_rad)" />
    <param name="joint_current_max_ma" value="$(arg joint_current_max_ma)" />
    <param name="joint_current_max_s" value="$(arg joint_current_max_s)" />
    <param name="start_att_max_deg"  value="$(arg start_att_max_deg)" />
    <param name="thruster_scale"     value="$(arg thruster_scale)" />
    <param name="thruster_max_s"     value="$(arg thruster_max_s)" />
    <param name="imu_yaw_offset_deg" value="$(arg imu_yaw_offset_deg)" />
    <param name="sensor_timeout_s"   value="$(arg sensor_timeout_s)" />
    <param name="joint_timeout_s"    value="$(arg joint_timeout_s)" />
    <!-- weights_dir defaults to ../numpy_port relative to the node file -->
  </node>

  <!-- The mixer is NOT optional. rl_inference_node publishes sim-ordered commands on
       /albc/thruster_cmd, and nothing else in the graph subscribes to that topic, so
       without this node the thrusters sit silently at neutral and the run reads as a
       policy failure. This launch shipped without it and reproduced exactly that.
       No <param> overrides on purpose: thruster_order / thruster_sign / thruster_deadband
       come from thruster_mixer.py's own defaults, which test_deploy_constants.py pins
       against deployed_tam.json. A second copy of those constants in a launch file is a
       second thing to keep in sync, and that is the drift this repo keeps paying for. -->
  <group if="$(arg launch_mixer)">
    <node pkg="albc_rl" name="thruster_mixer" type="thruster_mixer.py" output="screen" />
  </group>
</launch>
